1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,048
Where does code go when more than one module needs it?

2
00:00:03,098 --> 00:00:10,248
Security rules and Keycloak calls are needed by every module that takes requests, not only by the partner module.

3
00:00:10,298 --> 00:00:14,256
Copied into each module, every fix would have to be made twice.

4
00:00:14,306 --> 00:00:21,984
So shared code lives in platform libraries, in one versioned platform build, next to the error and OTP libraries.

5
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:29,640
The security library secures any app: access tokens, realm roles, and refusals as problem details.

6
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:34,248
Its route rules are configuration, and anything not listed is refused.

7
00:00:34,298 --> 00:00:39,624
It configures itself before Spring Boot's own defaults, and an app can still replace it.

8
00:00:39,674 --> 00:00:47,328
A module adds its own filter to that one chain through a customizer, and a customizer that sets route rules stops startup.

9
00:00:47,378 --> 00:00:53,784
The Keycloak library is a small admin client that logs in as its own client and refreshes its token.

10
00:00:53,834 --> 00:01:03,912
Every call has a configured timeout, a refused token is replaced once, and each failure is a problem detail: conflict, rejected, or unavailable.

11
00:01:03,962 --> 00:01:11,928
The partner module keeps its own identity port, and its Keycloak adapter delegates to that client, and turns a conflict into email taken.

12
00:01:11,978 --> 00:01:19,296
Each library ships its own defaults and error messages, so the app configures only what differs, and secrets come from Vault.

13
00:01:19,346 --> 00:01:25,608
A module asks the version catalog for a library by name, and Gradle builds it from the included platform build.

14
00:01:25,658 --> 00:01:29,544
Next, the money rules begin, with the commission on every payment.
